Lack of multi-factor authentication:
  A lack of multi-factor authentication is a vulnerable security implementation that can make it much easier for hackers/ identity theives to gain access to an account.
  An example of a company/ system not having multi-factor authentication installed is funko.com. While the option to enable multi-factor authentication in the Funko webiste
  exists, it is not enabled by default. This was done to enable the user to choose between authenticator apps or emails. While well meaning, the choice should be made 
  for the user (default to email with the ability to change later). This could lead to large groups of people forgetting to enable it, or not realising they can enable 
  it. The Funko website has the ability to handle online payments and orders, making it a high-risk service, which should have default multi-factor authentication. 
Unpatched/ outdated software:
  Unpatched/ outdated software puts devices at risk because they still contain publically documented vulnerabilities that have been found by the public. If a hacker knew
  the software version of the device, which with older devices can be guessed to a fairly certain degree, they know which publically documented vulnerabilities are in that
  device that haven't been patched. An example is my old i-pad. I have an i-pad that is too old to update to the latest ios, so is vulnerable to a host of issues that are
  patched in later updates.
Insecure network configuration (open ports):
  Insecure system configuration is caused by unnecessarily open ports, poorly segregated networks and disabled security features. These all make the server/ network more
  susceptible to unauthorised access or increased risk of malware propagation, which leads to data breaches. 
  Source: Palo Alto Networks. (2026). What is insecure system configuration? https://www.paloaltonetworks.com.au/cyberpedia/insecure-system-configuration-cicd-sec7
Poorly managed user permissions:
  Poorly managed user permissions are when user/ groups of users have default or elevated permissions when they don't need to or shouldn't have them. This directly affects
  the confidentiality part of the CIA triad, because people are able to access files and systems that they aren't supposed to. When a company cannot control who has access 
  to documents and to networks, they cannot ensure their security, because they cannot ensure that vital systems cannot be altered by someone who either doesn't know what
  they're doing, or has alternative reasons for doing it.
Inadequate input validation:
  Inadequate input validation, in cases when there are files being opened or queries being performed, is when a user provided input is not sufficiently vetted to ensure it
  doesn't try to open something it shouldn't. For example, if a website used a database to store login/ password information, the username provided by the user would need 
  to be properly validated to ensure it didn't try and access admin accounts or reveal the passwords for other accounts. In the case of websites opening files, for example,
  images, if the file path provided by a user (in the URL) isn't validated, it could open/ access a file, like passwd, and reveal sensitive information.
